#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162882} :activity/id #uuid "4407446b-5fc6-5ec1-a901-75a95c2caa7b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048871 :topic/id #uuid "c80985c0-d51a-5f21-9b71-21587d8d82cc" :topic/math-academy-id 3024} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163239 :tutorial/content "If $X$ is a continuous random variable and $0 \\leq p \\leq 1,$ then the $100p$th **percentile** is a number $x_p$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq x_p) = p.\n$$\n\n- In terms of CDF, the *cumulative distribution function* $F(x)$ of $X,$ the $100p$th percentile $x_p$ is a number on the $x$-axis such that\n\n$$\nF(x_p) = p.\n$$\n\n- In terms of PDF, the *probability density function* $f(x)$ of $X,$ the $100p$th percentile $x_p$ is a number on the $x$-axis such that the area under the graph of $y=f(x)$ to the left of $x$ is equal to $p \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{x_p} f(x) \\, \\text{d}x = p.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the $4$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/69/69a9f969e3caaa3cc3d896b5a8b6ed566cff3517743519f0c1088e588c3967e7.svg)\n\nThe $4$th percentile corresponds to the decimal $p={\\color{red}0.04}$ since $100 \\cdot 0.04 = 4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.04}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{x}_{0.04}}\\frac{1}{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}|}_{0}^{{x}_{0.04}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{{x}_{0.04}}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $4$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{{x}_{0.04}}^{2}}{4} & =0.04 \\\\ {{x}_{0.04}}^{2} & =0.16 \\\\ {x}_{0.04} & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0 \\leq x_{0.04} \\leq 2.$\n\nThere are a few special percentiles:\n\n- the $25$th percentile is called the **lower quartile**. This is the number $q_1$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq q_1) = 0.25.\n$$\n\n- the $50$th percentile is called the **median**. This is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq m) = 0.5.\n$$\n\n- the $75$th percentile is called the **upper quartile**. This is the number $q_3$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq q_3) = 0.75.\n$$\n\n- the **interquartile range** is the difference between the upper and lower quartiles, that is,\n\n$$\nIQR = q_3 - q_1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e91993d1-ae51-5742-8eeb-e76348c0b8af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4942 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b587e6fa-53fb-5f03-9ffa-373892fc4d10" :step/math-academy-id 30817 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161997 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161789 :question/id #uuid "dd626772-dd96-5840-8375-9ace79fecf70" :question/math-academy-id "e-8237"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c1cf7c-e334-5173-8ae4-86af0dce56ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482591}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Median Given the CDF a Continuous Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "6d1c73ec-5860-5de5-9ee3-0fa09198d61f" :step/math-academy-id 30818 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161998 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161790 :question/id #uuid "d22e7154-ac3c-5ac6-ad86-77a7eb08f82e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8238"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a79c4207-c5d6-5386-818e-a7e532492732" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482548} {: